.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/wrap-ansi/node_modules/ansi-styles Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "ansi-styles"
.PP
.RS 4
\fRANSI escape codes\fP for styling strings in the terminal 
.RE
.PP

.PP
You probably want the higher-level \fRchalk\fP module for styling your strings\&.

.PP
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install ansi\-styles
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
import styles from 'ansi\-styles';

console\&.log(`${styles\&.green\&.open}Hello world!${styles\&.green\&.close}`);


// Color conversion between 256/truecolor
// NOTE: When converting from truecolor to 256 colors, the original color
//       may be degraded to fit the new color palette\&. This means terminals
//       that do not support 16 million colors will best\-match the
//       original color\&.
console\&.log(`${styles\&.color\&.ansi(styles\&.rgbToAnsi(199, 20, 250))}Hello World${styles\&.color\&.close}`)
console\&.log(`${styles\&.color\&.ansi256(styles\&.rgbToAnsi256(199, 20, 250))}Hello World${styles\&.color\&.close}`)
console\&.log(`${styles\&.color\&.ansi16m(\&.\&.\&.styles\&.hexToRgb('#abcdef'))}Hello World${styles\&.color\&.close}`)
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "\fRopen\fP and \fRclose\fP"
Each style has an \fRopen\fP and \fRclose\fP property\&.
.SS "\fRmodifierNames\fP, \fRforegroundColorNames\fP, \fRbackgroundColorNames\fP, and \fRcolorNames\fP"
All supported style strings are exposed as an array of strings for convenience\&. \fRcolorNames\fP is the combination of \fRforegroundColorNames\fP and \fRbackgroundColorNames\fP\&.

.PP
This can be useful if you need to validate input:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import {modifierNames, foregroundColorNames} from 'ansi\-styles';

console\&.log(modifierNames\&.includes('bold'));
//=> true

console\&.log(foregroundColorNames\&.includes('pink'));
//=> false
.fi
.PP
