.SH "Advanced usage"
.PP
By default, you get a map of styles, but the styles are also available as groups\&. They are non-enumerable so they don't show up unless you access them explicitly\&. This makes it easier to expose only a subset in a higher-level module\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyles\&.modifier\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyles\&.color\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstyles\&.bgColor\fP
.PP
.SS "Example"
.PP
.nf
import styles from 'ansi\-styles';

console\&.log(styles\&.color\&.green\&.open);
.fi
.PP

.PP
Raw escape codes (i\&.e\&. without the CSI escape prefix \fR\\u001B[\fP and render mode postfix \fRm\fP) are available under \fRstyles\&.codes\fP, which returns a \fRMap\fP with the open codes as keys and close codes as values\&.
.SS "Example"
.PP
.nf
import styles from 'ansi\-styles';

console\&.log(styles\&.codes\&.get(36));
//=> 39
.fi
.PP
.SH "16 / 256 / 16 million (TrueColor) support"
.PP
\fRansi-styles\fP allows converting between various color formats and ANSI escapes, with support for 16, 256 and \fR16 million colors\fP\&.

.PP
The following color spaces are supported:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRrgb\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhex\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRansi256\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRansi\fP
.PP

.PP
To use these, call the associated conversion function with the intended output, for example:

.PP
.PP
.nf
import styles from 'ansi\-styles';

styles\&.color\&.ansi(styles\&.rgbToAnsi(100, 200, 15)); // RGB to 16 color ansi foreground code
styles\&.bgColor\&.ansi(styles\&.hexToAnsi('#C0FFEE')); // HEX to 16 color ansi foreground code

styles\&.color\&.ansi256(styles\&.rgbToAnsi256(100, 200, 15)); // RGB to 256 color ansi foreground code
styles\&.bgColor\&.ansi256(styles\&.hexToAnsi256('#C0FFEE')); // HEX to 256 color ansi foreground code

styles\&.color\&.ansi16m(100, 200, 15); // RGB to 16 million color foreground code
styles\&.bgColor\&.ansi16m(\&.\&.\&.styles\&.hexToRgb('#C0FFEE')); // Hex (RGB) to 16 million color foreground code
.fi
.PP
